\begin{lemma}[Width audit: the wedge is in the modulator class]\label{lem:pawidth-wedge-modulator}
For every admissible $(e,j,m)$ the game $\mathrm{WD}(e,j,m)$ of \Cref{sec:wedge} is stopping and satisfies $\mu_{\max}(\mathrm{WD}(e,j,m))=2$, with modulator $X=\{v_1,v_2\}$; indeed its directed feedback vertex number is $2$, so $\mu_{\mathcal P}=2$ already for $\mathcal P$ the acyclic games. The same holds for $\mathrm{CC}(L,m)$ of \Cref{thm:hybrid-lower}. The family $H_m$ of \Cref{cor:slack-stalls} is $\max$-acyclic, so $\mu_{\max}(H_m)=0$ and \Cref{thm:kacyclic} applies to it outright.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Deleting $v_1,v_2$ from $\mathrm{WD}$ leaves the $a$-, $b$-, $c$- and $w$-chains and $H$, on which every edge increases the chain index or leaves for a sink, so the residue is acyclic; and $v_1\to a_{1,1}\to\dots\to a_{1,e}$ returns to $v_1$, so no single vertex meets every cycle through a Max vertex. The same two deletions work on $\mathrm{CC}$. In $H_m$ the only Max vertex $v\to(c_1,h)$ has no in-edges, so no cycle meets it. Verified in exact arithmetic at $(e,j,m)=(3,2,5),(4,3,6),(5,4,8),(6,5,9),(2,5,9)$, at $(L,m)=(2,2),(3,3),(4,4)$ and at $m=3,\dots,6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The modulator class does reach the mechanisms]\label{rem:pawidth-modulator-hard}
Consequently the analogue of \Cref{rem:escape-class} fails for \Cref{thm:modulator}: $\mathrm{WD}$ keeps the $Z$-seeded own-successor hybrid silent for $2^{\Omega(N)}$ rounds (\Cref{thm:wedge-proved}, \Cref{cor:wedge-count}) and lies in the class at $\mu=2$; $\mathrm{CC}$ and $H_m$ do the same at $\mu=2$ and $\mu=0$. The escape class is excluded from the stalls by a \emph{rate} (\Cref{thm:slack-vi-upper}), and the modulator class carries none.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Width audit: a path decomposition of $P_D$]\label{prop:pawidth-pd}
For every $D\ge1$ the vertex order
\[
 u,\;\bigl(X_d,\;k_{2D-2d+2},\;k_{2D-2d+1},\;Y_d,\;F_d,\;G_d\bigr)_{d=1}^{D}
\]
(omitting indices outside $1..2D-1$) has vertex separation $4$ in $\Gamma(P_D)$, so $\operatorname{pw}(P_D)\le4$; and $\operatorname{tw}(P_D)=\operatorname{pw}(P_D)=2,3,3,4$ for $D=1,2,3,4$ with $\operatorname{pw}(P_D)=4$ for $4\le D\le8$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Width audit: not every family here has small width]\label{prop:pawidth-fv}
$\mathrm{FV}(5)$ and $\mathrm{FV}_2(5)$ of \Cref{prop:fv-family} satisfy $\operatorname{tw}=\operatorname{pw}=6$, and $\operatorname{pw}(\mathrm{FV}(4))=\operatorname{pw}(\mathrm{FV}_2(4))=5$, so the width of the families of this document is not bounded by four; the \emph{hard} families $L_n,H_m,G_m,\mathrm{WD},\mathrm{CC}$ have width $3,2,2,3,3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[Width audit: \Cref{thm:modulator} needs the stopping hypothesis]\label{rem:pawidth-stopping}
Let $H$ be the payoff game with the single average non-terminal $v$ and both edges $v\to v$. Then $\val_H=\lfp(T_H)=0$, while $\mu_{\mathcal P}(H)=1$ with $X=\{v\}$ and $\mathcal P$ the acyclic payoff games, $F_X(\theta)=\theta$ for every $\theta$, so every grid point is a fixed point of the rounded cut map, \Cref{thm:tarski} returns the midpoint of the grid, and the final check $T_Hw=w$ passes there. The word \emph{stopping} must appear in the hypothesis of \Cref{thm:modulator}, not only in its consequences.
\end{remark}
